INT. SERVER ROOM ELEVATOR - NIGHT

Brushed steel. Humming cold. Floor indicator: 41. 42. 43. AGENT RAHEL YOHANNES, 30s, black suit, earpiece, one hand inside her jacket, watches the numbers climb while MR. CORNELIUS BELL, 60s, pinstriped, briefcase handcuffed to his wrist, stands at the opposite corner with a smile too relaxed for an elevator at 3 a.m., and neither of them looks at the other until the lights go out and the car stops between floors with a deep, deliberate clunk.

Dark. Emergency red. Breathing.

RAHEL
You pressed something.

BELL
I did not.

RAHEL
Your elbow.

BELL
My elbow has been against this wall since the lobby.

RAHEL
Then the building is on your side.

BELL
Buildings are never on anyone's side, Agent Yohannes. They're just tall.

RAHEL
You know my name.

BELL
I know everyone's name. It's most of my job.

RAHEL
(into earpiece, muttering)
Control, I've lost the lift. Repeat, lost the lift.

BELL
They can't hear you. This shaft is shielded.

RAHEL
By whom?

BELL
By me. I paid for it.

RAHEL
Mr. Bell, put the briefcase on the floor.

BELL
And if I don't?

RAHEL
(drawing, steady)
Then I'll find out how shielded you are.

BELL
Agent, the case is not what you think. It isn't data. It's a key.

RAHEL
To what?

BELL
To this elevator. To every elevator in the building. At six a.m., the doors open on forty-three floors of sleeping people, and every one of them will be locked inside.

RAHEL
(quiet, to herself)
Dear God.

BELL
Yes. Rather the point.

The red light pulses. The cable above them groans.
